; THE HEADER-MODEL WITNESS (SPEC §14).
;
; This probe was written to expose a defect and now witnesses the rule that
; closed it. Both readings matter, so both are recorded.
;
; WHAT IT FOUND (#122). GitHub sends `X-GitHub-Event: push`. An Oath handler
; asking for the header by the name GitHub documents got NOTHING; asking for
; `X-Github-Event` got the value, because the Go backend's adapter ran requests
; through net/http, which canonicalizes header keys. That transformation was
; invisible in the Oath source, in the type, and in the guarantee — and it failed
; OPEN in the shape that matters: a handler branching on
; `(== (header-or hs "X-GitHub-Event" "-") "ping")` compared against a header
; that never arrived, so the ping branch was dead and every ping took the other
; path. Nothing in the artifact's verdict would have mentioned it.
;
; WHAT IT WITNESSES NOW. SPEC §14.2 requires every backend to deliver header
; names ASCII-lowercased, ordered lexicographically, with repeats preserved and
; the authority present as `host` whatever the HTTP version called it. So the
; answer below is fixed by the specification rather than by one backend's
; library, and it is the same answer on any conformant backend and any HTTP
; version:
;
;   oath put apps/github-webhook/hdr-probe.oath
;   oath build hdr-probe -o /tmp/hdr-probe && OATH_HTTP_ADDR=:8898 /tmp/hdr-probe &
;   curl -sS -X POST localhost:8898/ -H 'X-GitHub-Event: push' -d '{}'
;   MISS-as-documented|MISS-go-canonical|push|localhost:8898
;
; The THIRD field is the one that hits, and that is the whole point: neither the
; spelling the vendor documents nor the spelling a Go library produces is what an
; Oath handler sees. Look header names up in lowercase, always.
;
; The FOURTH field is the second defect this probe found, which the issue had not
; named. net/http promotes `Host` out of the header map into `Request.Host` and
; removes it, so a backend passing its library's collection through unexamined
; drops a mandatory header — and a backend that did not would disagree with it.
; §14.2 REQ-HOST-IS-A-HEADER settles that: it is a header entry, spelled `host`.
;
; If a future backend, or a change to the handler adapter, stops satisfying §14,
; this prints something else and it is the BACKEND that is wrong — which is the
; difference between a specification and a comment describing an accident.
(defn hdr-probe [] [(r Request)] Response
  (Resp 200 (Nil [(Pair Str Str)])
    (str-bytes (join-with "|"
      (Cons [Str] (header-or (req-headers r) "X-GitHub-Event" "MISS-as-documented")
        (Cons [Str] (header-or (req-headers r) "X-Github-Event" "MISS-go-canonical")
          (Cons [Str] (header-or (req-headers r) "x-github-event" "MISS-lowercase")
            (Cons [Str] (header-or (req-headers r) "host" "MISS-host")
              (Nil [Str]))))))))
  (prop always-200 [(r Request)] (== (match (hdr-probe r) ((Resp s h b) s)) 200)))
